const faqs = [
  { q: 'How much does tankless water heater repair cost?',
    a: 'Most tankless repairs in Rocklin fall between $180 and $950. A flame rod clean, igniter or flow sensor sits at the low end. A fan motor, gas valve or control board sits in the middle. A leaking or cracked heat exchanger often makes replacement the more economical option, particularly on an older unit — we assess the model, warranty, parts availability and repair cost before recommending either. A straight descale and service is typically $200 to $450. You get the figure in writing before any work starts, and the diagnostic fee comes off the price if you go ahead.' },
  { q: 'Why does my tankless unit show an error code?',
    a: 'An error code tells you which safety check the unit failed — it is a direction, not a diagnosis. Ignition and flame codes point at gas supply, the igniter or a dirty flame rod. Overheat codes usually mean scale inside the heat exchanger or restricted flow. Flow sensor codes mean the unit cannot confirm water is moving. Venting codes mean the exhaust or the air intake is blocked. We read the code, then test the part it is pointing at.' },
  { q: 'How often should a tankless water heater be descaled?',
    a: 'It depends on water hardness, household usage and what the manufacturer specifies. Annually is a sensible default; a hard-working family unit on harder water may want it more often, and a softener or dedicated scale filter can stretch the interval. Some manufacturers make maintenance part of their warranty conditions, so check your model’s warranty documentation for the schedule that applies.' },
  { q: 'Why does my hot water go cold for a few seconds?',
    a: 'That is the cold-water sandwich. The water already sitting in the pipe arrives hot, the cooled slug behind it arrives next, and the burner needs a moment to fire and catch up. A short burst of this is normal tankless behavior. A long cold gap, or one that keeps repeating through a shower, usually means a slow igniter, a failing flow sensor, or scale slowing the heat transfer.' },
  { q: 'How long do tankless water heaters last?',
    a: 'Tankless water heaters commonly last around fifteen to twenty years, depending on installation quality, water conditions, usage and maintenance. The heat exchanger usually sets the limit, and a neglected unit on harder water can fall well short of that.' },
  { q: 'Can you repair any brand?',
    a: 'We service popular tankless water heater brands, including Rinnai, Navien, Noritz, Takagi, Rheem and Bosch. Condensing and non-condensing, indoor and outdoor, natural gas and propane. The casings and code tables differ between brands but the failure points and the diagnostic logic are the same. We are an independent repair company, not a brand dealer.' },
  { q: 'Is tankless repair worth it or should I replace?',
    a: 'Under twelve years old with a sound heat exchanger, repair almost always wins — the parts are a fraction of the cost of a new unit. Past fifteen years, with a cracked or leaking heat exchanger, or when the repair quote passes about half the price of new, replacement is the better value. We give you both numbers and let you choose.' },
];

const schema = [
