  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What Tankless Error Codes Actually Mean</h2>
      <p>
        Every brand numbers its faults differently, so the code itself is not worth
        memorising. What matters is the family of fault it belongs to. A code tells
        you which safety check the unit failed — it narrows the search, it does not
        finish it. Here is what each family really points at.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top:22px">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Ignition failure</h3>
          <p>The unit called for flame and never got one. Causes run from a partly closed gas valve or an undersized gas supply, to a worn igniter, to a pressure drop when another appliance fires. On propane it is often a supply problem rather than a unit problem.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Flame loss</h3>
          <p>It lit, then lost the flame signal. Usually a flame rod coated in deposits, a poor ground, condensate sitting where it should not be, or combustion air starved by a blocked intake screen.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Overheat / high limit</h3>
          <p>The unit measured a temperature it is not allowed to reach. Scale lining the heat exchanger is the usual cause — it insulates the water from the copper, so the metal runs hot while your shower runs cool. Restricted flow does the same thing.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Flow sensor fault</h3>
          <p>The unit cannot confirm water is moving through it. A sticking turbine, grit from the street main, a clogged inlet filter screen, or a genuinely dead sensor. We see these a lot after any work on the water main.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Venting or exhaust blockage</h3>
          <p>Air pressure readings are out of range. A blocked exhaust termination, a bird or wasp nest in the intake, a vent joint that has pulled apart, a tiring fan motor, or debris drifted against an outdoor unit. Treat these as real safety codes.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Scale / service reminder</h3>
          <p>Some units simply count operating hours and ask to be serviced. Others stay quiet until performance has already dropped. Either way the answer is a proper descale, not clearing the code.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        One thing we will not do is reset a code and leave. A reset makes the display
        look healthy for a day or two and then the same fault returns, usually at a
        worse moment. We find the reason the check failed first.
      </p>

      <h2>Hard Water in Placer County, and Why Descaling Matters</h2>
      <p>
        Mineral content in the local supply can contribute to scale build-up inside a tankless
        heater, and how much depends on the water hardness where you are, how hard the unit works
        and its maintenance history. Water in this part of Placer County often leaves visible
        deposits on fixtures and glass, and a tankless heat exchanger is the harshest place in
        the house for that water to end up. It gets pushed through narrow copper
        passages and heated hard and fast, which is exactly the condition that drops
        minerals out of solution onto hot metal.
      </p>
      <p>
        A scaled exchanger then produces a set of symptoms that look like separate
        problems. Flow through the unit drops, so the burner modulates strangely. Heat
        transfer worsens, so the copper runs hotter than the water and the overheat
        sensor trips. Gas consumption climbs for the same amount of hot water. The
        unit gets noisier. And eventually the exchanger itself fails, which is the one
        repair that normally ends the heater.
      </p>
      <p>
        A descale is a service, not a repair. We isolate the unit at its service
        valves, circulate a descaling solution through the exchanger long enough to
        genuinely dissolve the deposits rather than just rinse them, flush it clean,
        then clean the inlet filter screen and the combustion air intake while we are
        in there. Descaling frequency depends on water hardness, household usage and the manufacturer's recommendations; annual service is a sensible starting point, and some units need it more often. If your unit has never been
        descaled and it is more than three or four years old, that is where we start
        — and a surprising number of heaters that arrive on our list as broken need
        nothing else.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your unit does not have isolation valves fitted, we can add them. It is a
        small job that makes every future service faster and cheaper, and on a unit
        you intend to keep it pays for itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Cold-Water Sandwich: Normal, or a Fault?</h2>
      <p>
        You open the hot tap, get a few seconds of hot, then a slug of cold, then hot
        again. That is the cold-water sandwich. The water already sitting in the pipe
        reaches you first, the cooled water behind it arrives next, and the burner
        needs a moment to fire and catch up with the demand.
      </p>
      <p>
        A brief version of this is ordinary tankless behavior and no repair removes
        it completely — only a recirculation loop or a small buffer tank really
        softens it. A brief cold interruption can be perfectly normal; pipe distance, plumbing layout, flow rate
        and recirculation design all affect how long it lasts. Longer or increasingly frequent
        interruptions are worth investigating, and the usual culprits are: a slow igniter, a
        flow sensor misreading low demand, a scaled exchanger that cannot recover
        temperature quickly, or low-flow fixtures that barely reach the minimum
        activation flow the unit needs. All of those are repairable.
      </p>

      <h2>Short-Cycling and Other Odd Behavior</h2>
      <p>
        Short-cycling is a unit that fires, shuts down, and fires again within
        seconds, often with an audible thump from the burner. The usual causes are
        demand below the minimum activation flow — a trickling tap or a very
        water-efficient shower head — a thermostatic mixing valve bleeding cold into
        the hot line, scale restricting flow, or a flow sensor reporting noise rather
        than real movement. A recirculation pump left on an aggressive timer will also
        drive a unit into constant short bursts and shorten its life for no benefit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other symptoms worth a call: hot water that is strong at one fixture and weak
        at another, a unit that copes alone but drops out the moment the dishwasher
        starts, a rumble or kettling noise that is getting louder, or hot water that
        overshoots then dives. One symptom is an emergency rather than a repair call: if you smell gas or suspect carbon
        monoxide, leave the property immediately, do not operate electrical switches or use flames,
        and call 911 or your gas utility from a safe location. Call us afterwards, once you are safe.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the diagnosis points to replacement rather than repair, note that water heater
        replacement in Rocklin requires a plumbing permit — we confirm the applicable permit
        requirements before starting any replacement work.
      </p>

      <h2>Brands We Service in Rocklin</h2>
      <p>
        We work on <strong>Rinnai, Navien, Noritz, Takagi, Rheem and Bosch</strong>,
        the brands we see most often in Rocklin homes — from the early non-condensing heaters
        still running in Sunset Whitney garages to the condensing units going into Whitney Ranch
        builds. We are
        independent. No brand pays us and no brand decides what we recommend. Indoor
        and outdoor, natural gas and propane. The code tables differ, but flame rods,
        flow sensors, igniters, fan motors, gas valves and heat exchangers fail for
        the same reasons in all of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Descale, Repair or Replace?</h2>
      <p>
        Three different answers, and the honest one depends on three things: the age
        of the unit, whether the heat exchanger is sound, and what the repair costs
        against a new heater.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top:22px">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Descale</h3>
          <p>Performance has faded, overheat codes show up under heavy use, gas use has crept upward, but no part has actually failed. The cheapest fix there is, and often the only one needed.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Repair</h3>
          <p>A specific component has failed on a unit under about twelve years old with a healthy exchanger. Igniters, flame rods, flow sensors, fans, gas valves and boards are all worth replacing at that age.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Replace</h3>
          <p>Heat exchanger cracked or leaking, unit past fifteen years, parts no longer made, or a repair quote above half the price of new. <a href="/tankless-water-heater-replacement-rocklin">Tankless replacement →</a></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>When a Tankless Repair Is Not Worth It</h2>
      <p>
        We would rather sell you a repair — it is the easier job and the smaller
        disruption. But there are four situations where we will tell you to stop
        spending money on the unit you have.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A failed heat exchanger.</strong> It is the most expensive part in the
        heater, and on an older unit the labor to fit one approaches the cost of a
        new heater that arrives with a fresh warranty. If it is leaking internally,
        that is normally the end of the road.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Several parts failing in sequence.</strong> A board in the spring, a
        fan in the summer, a gas valve now. That is a unit telling you its service
        life is over, not a run of bad luck.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Parts no longer stocked.</strong> Discontinued models eventually hit a
        wall where the only boards available are used and unwarranted. We will not fit
        those into a gas appliance.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>It was never the right unit.</strong> Now and then we find an
        undersized heater that has spent its entire life flat out, or one fitted to an
        undersized gas line it never had a chance on. No repair fixes a specification
        problem — in that case what you want is the
        <a href="/tankless-water-heater-replacement-rocklin">replacement</a> page, and
        a unit sized properly this time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

